docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:19:The paper is honest and its numbers match its outputs. Its conclusions are narrower than its framing. The main result is a null: R3 and a history-based comparator differ by 0.021 in attacker gain. The "equivalent" label comes from a wide margin, and three things can change it: search error as large as the margin, variation between markets, and a comparator that was not selected on gain. The two Q1 "families" are mostly one test. The model has no honest churn, so identity age labels the attacker almost perfectly. The theory is correct but thin. The paper reads as an audit record, not as a finding.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:28:| 2 | R3 and C* are equivalent within ±0.05, R3 lower, under two attack families | Report Q1 lines 9 to 18 | **Narrower.** The point estimates reproduce. "Equivalent" depends on the margin, and "two families" is mostly one (problems 1, 2). |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:39:| Q1 mean gains (C*, R3) | 0.086, 0.065; 0.091, 0.067 | **0.0856, 0.0649; 0.0913, 0.0670, reproduced** | `stability-result.json`, `mean_old` |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:47:| Lottery, C* with H4 | 0.4845, 0.000456, 0.921 | **0.00044 and 0.921, reproduced** | Same method |
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:56:**1 → "Equivalent" is a margin effect, and three sources of error can change it.**
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:57:Quote (abstract): "the registered procedure classifies R3 and C* as 'equivalent, direction R3 lower' within the registered margin of ±0.05".
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:59:- **Search error.** The doubling check moves one cell by 0.054 (fresh3, C*, D2+H3; report line 225). That exceeds the margin.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:67:- X7-SC takes the D2+H3 winner as an incumbent (design 5.2). In 4 of 6 markets both the C* and R3 X7-SC cells have the same means as the D2+H3 cells (`stability-result.json`).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:74:- A 7-knob family (X5-AG/v2) beats the 15-knob X7-SC against C* on fresh1, fresh2, and fresh3 (report line 166).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:84:- Table 6 shows the cost: an honest replacement is audited at 92 to 93 percent after its window and earns about 4 percent less than under C* (2.74 against 2.86).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:86:- **Effect.** The G effect and the R3-against-C* result may not hold outside this model.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:89:**5 → C* was not selected on attacker gain.**
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:92:- C*'s score is 0.1032 (ledger 3.2). That is an efficiency of 0.993. Gain did not enter the choice (my reasoning).
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:93:- **Effect.** Q1 compares on gain against a comparator chosen on efficiency. A gain-selected comparator can change the direction label.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:99:- The real content is the check for B2 and B3. That is one parenthesis and three proof lines.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:136:The contribution is modest. Probation, credibility thresholds, and cheap-pseudonym costs are known, and the paper cites them fairly. The new parts are newest-first residual targeting under a hard audit quota and a registered comparison. The comparison is a null result.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:147:A reader gets lost in three places: the abstract (330 words, undefined labels), section 8 (calibration and block-hash detail before any result), and the prediction-eligibility text on pages 16 to 17.
docs/revision/roster/fable52-paper-r1.md:158:- The first test, except three sentences.
